<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Miyoshi clan domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1549 CE – 1575 CE (approx.) · capital Iimoriyama</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The regime of Miyoshi Nagayoshi, who controlled Kyoto and the home provinces in the 1550s, ruling central Japan before Oda Nobunaga.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Miyoshi were retainers of the Hosokawa deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a>, with a base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awa-province-tokushima">Awa</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. In 1549 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyoshi-nagayoshi">Miyoshi Nagayoshi</a> turned against his master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-harumoto">Hosokawa Harumoto</a>, defeated his forces at Eguchi and took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, driving the shogun into exile.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nagayoshi and his brothers controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinai">Kinai</a> and much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, ruling through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> when it suited them. From 1560 he made Iimoriyama castle his seat. Some historians regard his regime as the first true central power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a>, with direct control of the capital, its merchants and trade through <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sakai">Sakai</a>, and with close links to linked-verse poets and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> masters.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Nagayoshi died in 1564, his heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyoshi-yoshitsugu">Yoshitsugu</a>, the three Miyoshi elders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsunaga-hisahide">Matsunaga Hisahide's</a> son Hisamichi killed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiteru">Ashikaga Yoshiteru</a> in 1565 but then fought each other. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> drove them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1568; Yoshitsugu was killed in 1573, and the last resistance ended in 1575.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/miyoshi-clan-domain.jpg" alt="Miyoshi clan domain" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_crest_Sanngai_Hisi_ni_itutu_Kuginuki.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1549 CE – 1575 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Iimoriyama</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Miyoshi Nagayoshi, Miyoshi Yoshitsugu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Miyoshi clan, Miyoshi regime</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1562&amp;focus=miyoshi-clan-domain" aria-label="Miyoshi clan domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8182 879 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Miyoshi clan domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8448 1163h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8502 1135h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8502 1135h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Japan. <a href="/atlas?year=1562&amp;focus=miyoshi-clan-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

